###############################################################################
# KUL_VBG 2.0 — Apptainer definition
#
# This is a THIN WRAPPER over the Docker image, not a second recipe. The
# Dockerfile in this directory is the single source of truth for what gets
# installed; this file only adds Apptainer-native metadata (help text, labels,
# an explicit runscript) on top of it. Keeping one build recipe avoids the
# classic failure mode where a Docker image and a .def file drift apart and
# start producing subtly different results.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# You usually do NOT need this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apptainer converts a Docker image to a SIF directly, carrying over ENV and
# ENTRYPOINT, so the plain conversion is enough for almost everyone:
#
#     apptainer build KUL_VBG_2.0.sif docker-daemon://kul_vbg:2.0
#
# Use this .def only if you want `apptainer run-help` to print usage, or want
# the SIF labelled with its provenance.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Building
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Def-file builds run %post/%labels through a build sandbox, so they need
# --fakeroot (or root). On a machine where you have neither, use the plain
# conversion above instead — it needs no privileges at all.
#
#     apptainer build --fakeroot KUL_VBG_2.0.sif KUL_VBG.def
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bootstrap source
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker-daemon pulls from the local Docker daemon, i.e. the image you just
# built with build.sh. Two alternatives, depending on where you are building:
#
#   * From a registry (needs outbound network):
#         Bootstrap: docker
#         From: radwankul/kul_vbg:2.0
#
#   * From a tarball, for an air-gapped machine
#     (`docker save kul_vbg:2.0 -o kul_vbg_2.0.tar` on the machine that has it):
#         Bootstrap: docker-archive
#         From: kul_vbg_2.0.tar
###############################################################################

Bootstrap: docker-daemon
From: kul_vbg:2.0

%labels
    Maintainer      radwanphd@gmail.com
    Software        KUL_VBG
    Version         2.0
    Description     Virtual Brain Grafting — whole-brain parcellation in the presence of large lesions
    Source          https://github.com/KUL-Radneuron/KUL_VBG
    Reference       https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2021.117731
    License         MPL-2.0

%environment
    # The Docker image's ENV is inherited automatically by the bootstrap, so
    # this block is intentionally near-empty. Do NOT re-export FREESURFER_HOME,
    # FSLDIR, ANTSPATH or PATH here: entrypoint.sh already pins them, and doing
    # it twice creates two places to keep in sync when a version is bumped.
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export LANG=C.UTF-8

%runscript
    # Hand straight to the same entrypoint the Docker image uses, so `docker
    # run <img> <cmd>` and `apptainer run <sif> <cmd>` behave identically.
    exec /entrypoint.sh "$@"

%help
    KUL_VBG 2.0 — Virtual Brain Grafting
    ====================================

    Whole-brain parcellation in the presence of large brain lesions: the lesion
    is excised and replaced with healthy-looking synthetic tissue, so that
    FreeSurfer/FastSurfer can parcellate a brain they would otherwise fail on.

    Please cite:
      Radwan AM, Emsell L, Blommaert J, et al. Virtual brain grafting: Enabling
      whole brain parcellation in the presence of large lesions. NeuroImage
      2021;229:117731. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2021.117731

    REQUIRED AT RUN TIME
    --------------------
    A FreeSurfer licence. It is NOT included in this image — the licence is
    per-user and cannot be redistributed. Get one free at
    https://surfer.nmr.mgh.harvard.edu/registration.html and bind it in:

        -B /path/to/license.txt:/licence/license.txt

    USAGE
    -----
    Help:
        apptainer run -B /path/to/license.txt:/licence/license.txt \
            KUL_VBG_2.0.sif KUL_VBG.sh -h

    A typical run (data in $PWD, GPU present):
        apptainer run --nv \
            -B /path/to/license.txt:/licence/license.txt \
            -B "$PWD":/data \
            KUL_VBG_2.0.sif \
            KUL_VBG.sh -S PAT001 -a /data/sub-PAT001_T1w.nii.gz \
                       -l /data/lesion.nii.gz -z T1 \
                       -o /data/VBG_out -B 1 -P 1 -n 8 -v

    Without a GPU, drop --nv. FastSurfer (-P 2/3) then runs on CPU: slower,
    but correct. -P 1 (recon-all) and -P 4 (SynthSeg) are unaffected.

    An interactive shell inside the container:
        apptainer shell -B /path/to/license.txt:/licence/license.txt \
            -B "$PWD":/data KUL_VBG_2.0.sif

    NOTES
    -----
      * Bind your data to /data. Paths you pass to KUL_VBG.sh must be the
        paths as seen INSIDE the container (/data/...), not host paths.
      * Consider --cleanenv. Apptainer merges the host environment by default;
        entrypoint.sh already pins the variables that matter, but --cleanenv
        removes a whole class of surprises on a server where users have their
        own FSLDIR/FREESURFER_HOME exported.
      * /tmp must be writable — KUL_VBG works around a FreeSurfer 8.2.0
        mris_register bug by routing SUBJECTS_DIR through a short symlink
        there. If your site mounts /tmp read-only, bind over it:
        -B /scratch/$USER/tmp:/tmp
      * Contents: FreeSurfer 8.2.0, FSL 6.0.7.23 (subset), ANTs 2.4.4.post20,
        MRtrix3 (CLI only), FastSurfer, and KUL_VBG 2.0.
